g  it within a multiplex assay  so here we're going to talk about  something that we call the umbrella  effect of dav  and dab we we think inhibits staining of  other markers in close proximity  uh by basically creating a molecular  shell  so what you can see here is if we try  and stain our marker  with dab you can see it creates the the  dab  deposits around that particular marker  and next to it we have another  antibody which isn't in such close  proximity  and we can come in and we can stain that  effectively  so the left-hand side of the the dab  marker  you can see a third and the molecular  shell that we say with the the dab  can actually prevent that particular  marker coming in to bind effectively  with the staining images on this picture  as well what you can see  is that just by alternating the position  of the dab stain we can actually  improve the quality of the staining so  on the left hand side  we've used dab as the first marker for  cd20  and it's very difficult here to see the  cd30 and silver  and just by swapping these over and  putting cd30 first  cd 22nd you can see that the the  staining is a much more apparent  and just these changes in sequence can  have a big impact on  chromogenic multiplexing  so here we really need to consider  consider market localization  um ideally and this isn't always going  to happen  um it would be optimal to consider  markers that don't overlap  this is quite difficult in most cases so  if you do need to choose markers that  are  are going to overlap it's important to  choose the correct chromogens that you  may wish to use  so here we can see by using as ki-67 in  yellow and cd20 with purple  it's very easy to see where the  co-localization takes place  however below where we have cd8 in  purple  cd3 and silver it's very difficult we  just get a  almost a black stain and it's very  difficult to see where those two  different markers are  so here we have an example of this  chromogen sequence  and changing these