u're  looking at these markers  and the way in which you're applying the  different chromogens to get the best  possible staining  so to summarize these um detection tips  that we've  uh mentioned and this is a recommended  chromogen sequence that we've put  together  and as you can see silver really can be  inserted anywhere within the sequence  but it's really dependent on the type of  neutralization method you may be using  if you are using h2o2 neutralization  then it's important to use this right at  the end of the sequence  also consider the masking effect of dab  particularly in co-localized markers and  how  dab could interfere with the staining of  these different  markers within the tissue red we've  found does get more pink  if it's used in later steps within the  sequence  and you definitely get a better contrast  between the red and purple  if it's applied early in the sequence  particularly before yellow and teal  as i mentioned with silver it's  important to use it at the end of the  sequence if you are using neutralization  and also teal we found does fade a  little bit if it's a  prolonged exposure to  so those are a couple of tips to  consider  so i also want to touch upon some of the  fluorescent options that we have  available for the discovery ultra as  well  uh around two uh two and a half years  ago we launched the discovery five plex  immunofluorescence procedure and with  the  iaf procedure what this offers is a more  robust  pre-optimized procedure whereby the  customer can come in  with their primary antibodies and just  excuse me apply these primary antibodies  and we've already preset the detection  order  uh and with the the uh neutralization  and denaturing steps pre-applied  so this is a lot more easy to use uh by  applying the ready-to-use products that  we have available  the customers have pretty much a plug  and play  option available to them to create a  fluorescent five plex assay  these are what the hrp fluorophores that  that 