n  by h202 which is quite commonly used  within these types of assays  these are a couple of the detection  methods that we have available  for the discovery so this is an  anti-species or anti-hatan approach with  enzyme  deposited chromogens on the left hand  side we have the multimode detection  which basically is a an anti-species  secondary antibody and conjugated to  that  is your enzyme which is going to drive  the chromogenic reaction  and in this case it can be either hrp or  ap  on the right hand side we have a hapten  detection method  and here what we use is a proprietary  product created at roche tissue  diagnostics  whereby we conjugate a hapten or a  synthetic  label to a secondary antibody so in this  case we use hq or np  and then we come in with an antibody  directed against these synthetic  labels so we come in with an anti-hq hrp  or an anti-np-ap with this  we really see a huge increase in  specificity  sensitive sensitivity and signal  intensity  and we can use this method with  chromogens or fluorophores  and these are excellent for detecting  low expressing targets  so once we've decided on the type of  detection method that we're going to  implement  we recommend not diving straight into a  multiplex assay what we advise is  optimizing each antibody individually  so once you've decided on the panel that  you're creating  optimize each of those individually work  out which  marker is going to work best with each  detection type with  each chromogen type and then create your  staining reference library so you've  always got something to go back to  in order to determine uh if that stain  is working effectively if you were to  dive straight into a  four or five plex assay it's very  difficult to work out what may or may  not have gone wrong with the assay if  there are problems  at the end of the the staining ground by  having that reference library  you can go back and ensure that  everything is working effectively in  each of the different detection methods  one of the biggest issues we find with  multiplexing assays is actually creating  an assay where  the different markers are able to be  configured with their different antigen  retrieval methods  so in some cases different markers react  differently to different types of  antigen retrieval  and it's important that we try and find  some common ground so trying to find a  retrieval method which is going to suit  all the different antigens within that  particular tissue  section with the software that we have  available for the discovery what we can  offer is progressive retrieval  and what we mean by that is basically  being able to add  additional retrieval as we go through  the the procedure  so you may start off with your in your  first sequence by staining something  which has a very light antigen retrieval  and then towards the end of the the  sequence we've  added or progressively added more  retrieval to hits those additional  antigens throughout the sequences  the two key main the two main types of  retrieval that we  use or our customers use would be either  a heat retrieval or enzymatic retrieval  and as i mentioned with with heat try  and find a method that's efficient for  all the antigens  we can add additional heat applications  through each sequences  and then we can also use different types  of retrieval buffers so in terms of  a high or a low ph retrieval buffer for  each of the different markers  enzymatic retrieval can be a little bit  problematic because it can have a  negative effect  on other targets within the tissue so in  this case if you do have to use an  enzyme we always recommend using that in  the final sequence so you're not going  to affect any of the other markers  within that particular multiplex assay  as i mentioned as well we we offer  either sequential  or cocktailed uh multiplexing options  so with the sequential method uh what we  do is we actually apply each antibody in  detection  individually and then we move